MODIFICATIONS relative to the upstream branch above
---------------------------------------------------
Every file under `crates/buzz/` is a byte-verbatim copy of the upstream source
EXCEPT the four changes below. The layout mirrors the upstream repo root (its
`crates/`, `migrations/`, `Dockerfile`, `LICENSE`) so `git diff` against
block/buzz stays meaningful and upstream commits remain scannable/pullable.

1. `Cargo.toml` — workspace `members` trimmed from the upstream ~27-crate set to
   the 12-crate transitive `[dependencies]` closure of `buzz-relay`, so the
   relay compiles standalone. `exclude = ["desktop/src-tauri"]` dropped (desktop
   not vendored). No dependency versions or `[patch.crates-io]` entries changed.

2. `rust-toolchain.toml` (upstream pins channel 1.95.0) intentionally NOT
   vendored — maxplayerai supplies the Rust toolchain via nix. NOTE: the relay's
   sqlx 0.9 requires rustc >= 1.94, which is newer than the maxplayerai nix
   devShell default (nixos-25.11 -> 1.91.1); the build toolchain must provide
   >= 1.94.

3. `crates/buzz-relay/src/handlers/ingest.rs` — two additions, each marked inline
   with a comment:
   (a) the kind superset from maxplayer-core (formerly attributed to the Mobee crate): `KIND_MOBEE_*` consts plus
       `required_scope_for_kind` arms — the trade block 3401-3406 and the seller
       heartbeat 30340 -> `MessagesWrite`; the self-describing discovery/config
       kinds (NIP-89 handler 31990, NIP-17 DM-relay list 10050) -> `UsersWrite`
       (kind-0's class) — with the scope test rewritten to assert the mobee
       numbers. The fork's original DVM arms (3400/5109/6109/7000) are untouched.
   (b) the MakePrisms/maxplayerai #397 payment/DoS bound on NIP-17 events: the
       gift wrap (1059) and the DM-relay list (10050) are capped at 128 KB of
       content, and the gift wrap must additionally carry at least one `p` tag.

4. Git push tokens — the maxplayerai seller delivery path. A seller delivers a hired
   job as a git push to the relay, authenticated with a NIP-98 (kind:27235) bearer
   token. Two additions serve that path, each marked inline with a comment:
   (a) `crates/buzz-relay/src/api/git/{transport.rs,hook.rs,policy.rs}` (PR #929) — a
       push token MAY carry one `["ref", "<refname>"]` tag naming the single ref it may
       write. `transport.rs` reads the FIRST such tag after signature verification, the
       pre-receive hook carries it in its environment, its length-prefixed HMAC input,
       and its callback body, and the policy endpoint denies the push (403) when any ref
       update is not an EXACT match. A token with no `ref` tag keeps today's behaviour.
   (b) `crates/buzz-auth/src/nip98.rs` plus `crates/buzz-relay/src/{config.rs,nip11.rs}`
       and the transport call site — a scope-conditional freshness rule.
       `verify_nip98_event_with_policy` grants a scoped token that also carries a NIP-40
       `["expiration", <unix>]` tag a life up to
       `BUZZ_SCOPED_TOKEN_MAX_LIFETIME_SECS` (default 21600 s = 6 h), and rejects one
       that asks for more. `verify_nip98_event` keeps the ±60 s window, so every caller
       outside the git transport is unchanged. The effective cap is advertised in the
       NIP-11 `limitation` object as `scoped_token_max_lifetime_secs`. The ref-name
       validator moved from `policy.rs` to `buzz-auth` so one definition serves both the
       freshness rule and the push-time enforcement. While the cap is non-zero the git
       legs also refuse a `ref` tag that is not a valid ref name rather than reading it
       as an absent scope.
   No other source (`.rs`) files were modified.

Vendored (the 12-crate relay build closure): buzz-relay, buzz-core,
buzz-conformance, buzz-db, buzz-pubsub, buzz-auth, buzz-search, buzz-audit,
buzz-workflow, buzz-media, buzz-sdk, buzz-relay-mesh.

Left behind (not in the relay's build closure): the AI-agent harness (buzz-acp,
buzz-agent, buzz-dev-mcp, buzz-persona), device pairing (buzz-pair-relay,
buzz-pairing-cli), desktop / mobile / web clients, CLIs (buzz-cli, buzz-admin),
buzz-push-gateway, buzz-test-client, buzz-ws-client, sprig, the git-*-nostr
credential helpers, and examples/countdown-bot.
